# clang-tidy configuration for lcc. Run with scripts/tidy.sh.
#
# The check list is curated rather than "all of readability-* and modernize-*",
# because several upstream checks fight this project's goal of being read by
# people learning compilers. Each disabled family is listed with its reason, so
# the exclusions can be re-argued rather than rediscovered.
#
# Only one check is taken from the google- family: google-explicit-constructor.
# AST nodes are always built as `new Node(child)` from the grammar actions, so
# no constructor in ast/Nodes.hpp is meant to convert; Constant alone declared
# eight single-argument ones, over char, int, long, float, double and their
# unsigned variants. The rest of that family is naming and formatting rules
# this project does not follow.
#
# Note what the check does not reach: it flags only constructors callable with
# exactly one argument. The headers also mark the multi-argument constructors
# explicit, because C++11 lets those fire implicitly through
# copy-list-initialization -- `f({lhs, rhs})` and `Add a = {lhs, rhs}` both
# select one. No check enforces that half, so it falls to review.
#
# One check is taken from the cppcoreguidelines- family:
# cppcoreguidelines-virtual-class-destructor. Every base here is deleted
# polymorphically through Node* or TypeEnv*, so each one needs a public virtual
# destructor; the check holds that. Note that a different check from the same
# family is deliberately off for the opposite reason -- see
# cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions below.
#
# Deliberately NOT enabled:
#
#   modernize-use-trailing-return-type
#       Would rewrite 358 signatures into `auto f() -> llvm::Value*`. That form
#       is harder to skim for a reader who is here to learn compilers, not
#       modern C++ syntax, and it buries the return type that these APIs are
#       largely about.
#   readability-identifier-length
#       Flags `i`, `f`, `pb`, `mpm`. Conventional loop and builder names.
#   readability-magic-numbers
#       Nearly every hit is a bit width (8/16/32/64) or a DWARF constant, where
#       the literal is clearer than a named constant would be.
#   readability-function-cognitive-complexity
#       The AST walkers are inherently branchy; splitting them to satisfy a
#       threshold would scatter logic that reads better in one place.
#   bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#       Fires on every (builder, lhs, rhs, lhsTypeId, rhsTypeId) signature in
#       the operator lowering, which is the shape the domain calls for.
#   performance-inefficient-string-concatenation
#       Fires in dot/AstToDot.cpp, where `tree += a + " [label = " + b + "]\n"`
#       reads as the DOT line it produces. Splitting each into five `+=` calls
#       to avoid temporaries would obscure that, and this runs once per AST node
#       only under -v, so it is not a path worth optimizing for.
#   cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions
#       Reports every one of the node classes for declaring a destructor
#       without copy operations. The hazard is real, and it is fixed at the
#       root instead: ast/Nodes.hpp deletes Node's copy constructor and copy
#       assignment, which makes every node below it non-copyable and every
#       shallow copy a compile error. The check is per-class and syntactic, so
#       it goes on reporting derived classes regardless; satisfying it would
#       mean repeating four declarations in each of them.
#   modernize-use-nodiscard
#       Wants [[nodiscard]] on all ~180 const member functions, overrides
#       included. The attribute is applied here on the declaring base only,
#       which is where the contract belongs and is sufficient: every call in
#       this tree dispatches through Node*, Expr*, or VarType*, and a call
#       through the base warns whether or not the override repeats it. The
#       remaining findings would be 160 lines of annotation buying nothing.
#   misc-const-correctness
#       Applied to the tree by hand, but not enforced. The check cannot tell a
#       value from an RAII guard, and wants `const ScopedSymbolTable scope(...)`
#       on every one of them. Those objects are never read: they exist for their
#       constructor and destructor, and const on one reads as a claim about a
#       value that nobody was going to use. The findings that really were
#       values are const; leaving the guards bare costs the enforcement and
#       keeps the declarations saying what they do.
#   readability-use-anyofallof
#       Would replace a four-line `for` in DeclToIr.cpp with std::any_of and a
#       lambda. The loop is the more approachable form for this audience.
#
# Bug-hunting checks (bugprone-*, clang-analyzer-*) are intentionally out of
# scope here; this configuration is about style and redundancy only.

Checks: >
  -*,
  cppcoreguidelines-virtual-class-destructor,
  google-explicit-constructor,
  modernize-deprecated-headers,
  modernize-use-default-member-init,
  modernize-use-auto,
  modernize-use-bool-literals,
  modernize-use-equals-default,
  modernize-use-nullptr,
  modernize-use-override,
  performance-avoid-endl,
  performance-inefficient-vector-operation,
  performance-trivially-destructible,
  readability-const-return-type,
  readability-container-size-empty,
  readability-convert-member-functions-to-static,
  readability-else-after-return,
  readability-implicit-bool-conversion,
  readability-make-member-function-const,
  readability-math-missing-parentheses,
  readability-named-parameter,
  readability-non-const-parameter,
  readability-qualified-auto,
  readability-redundant-declaration,
  readability-redundant-member-init,
  readability-redundant-string-cstr,
  readability-simplify-boolean-expr,
  readability-static-definition-in-anonymous-namespace

# Never analyze flex/bison output; it is machine-generated and regenerated on
# every configure.
#
# Two keys rather than one negative-lookahead pattern. clang-tidy matches these
# with llvm::Regex, which is POSIX ERE and has no lookahead, and a pattern it
# cannot compile does not fail the run: llvm::Regex::match() returns false for
# every path instead. The earlier 'src/(?!generated/).*\.hpp$' therefore
# suppressed every header diagnostic rather than excluding one directory, and
# so reported clean while analyzing no header at all. Check any change to these
# two lines by confirming a deliberate slip in a .hpp is still reported.
HeaderFilterRegex: 'src/.*\.hpp$'
ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex: 'src/generated/.*'

WarningsAsErrors: ''
FormatStyle: file

# modernize-use-default-member-init writes `T* p{nullptr}` by default. Every
# member initializer already in this tree uses assignment -- SourceLoc, the
# Symbol variant, the option structs -- so pin the option rather than let the
# check introduce a second spelling.
CheckOptions:
  modernize-use-default-member-init.UseAssignment: true
